🔐 Hydra

🎯 Purpose

Hydra is a fast, parallelized network login password-cracking tool. It allows security auditors and penetration testers to test authentication strength across numerous network protocols by performing dictionary attacks against remote login interfaces.

🧠 Key Concepts

Online Password Cracking: Unlike offline tools (which analyze stored hashes), Hydra interacts directly with running network services by sending live login attempts.

Protocol Flexibility: Supports a wide variety of protocols, including SSH, FTP, HTTP/HTTPS (GET, POST, Basic, Form-based), SMB, RDP, MySQL, Telnet, and VNC.

Parallel Execution / Multithreading: Executes multiple concurrent tasks (threads) across connections to increase attack speed.

Rate Limiting & Lockouts: Online attacks run the risk of triggering account lockout policies, generating high volumes of audit logs, or causing Denial of Service (DoS) conditions on target services.

💻 Commands

# Basic SSH Attack with Single User and Password List
hydra -l admin -P /usr/share/wordlists/rockyou.txt ssh://192.168.1.10

# Attack FTP Using Username List and Password List
hydra -L users.txt -P passwords.txt ftp://192.168.1.10

# Specify Number of Parallel Tasks/Threads (-t)
hydra -l user -P passwords.txt -t 4 ssh://192.168.1.10

# Target a Non-Standard Port (-s)
hydra -l admin -P passwords.txt -s 2222 ssh://192.168.1.10

# Exit Immediately Upon Finding First Valid Credential (-f)
hydra -l admin -P passwords.txt -f 192.168.1.10 ssh



📋 Examples

SSH Login Audit:

hydra -l root -P /usr/share/wordlists/rockyou.txt -t 4 -V 10.10.10.20 ssh



HTTP POST Web Form Login Audit:

hydra -l admin -P passwords.txt 10.10.10.50 http-post-form "/login.php:user=^USER^&pass=^PASS^:Login failed"



🔎 Investigation

Monitor Failed Authentication Events: Search central SIEM and authentication logs (e.g., Windows Event ID 4625, Linux /var/log/auth.log) for rapid bursts of failed login attempts originating from a single IP address.

Identify Account Lockout Spikes: Detect potential brute-force or credential-stuffing campaigns by observing widespread account lockout triggers across network services.

⚠️ Important

Account Lockout Risk: Executing Hydra against active enterprise systems can inadvertently lock out legitimate user accounts.

Network Noise: Generating thousands of network authentication requests creates significant log telemetry and will quickly trigger Network Intrusion Detection Systems (NIDS).

Authorization: Performing online brute-force attacks against systems without explicit authorization is illegal.

🛡️ Security

Implement strict Account Lockout and Rate Limiting policies to mitigate automated brute-force attempts.

Enforce Multi-Factor Authentication (MFA) across all external-facing and internal authentication services.

Deploy Fail2ban or equivalent automated IP blocking tools to restrict clients that exceed failed login thresholds.

